    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Type 2 diabetes treatment options include lifestyle modifications, oral and injectable medications, and insulin therapy to manage blood sugar levels and prevent complications.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "lifestyle modifications, oral and injectable medications, and insulin therapy"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "1. Lifestyle Modifications (Foundational Care)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Dietary Changes: Focus on balanced meals, managing carbohydrate intake, and working with a dietitian to control blood sugar and support weight management.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Physical Activity: Incorporate regular aerobic, resistance, and flexibility exercises (such as brisk walking or strength training) to improve insulin sensitivity."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Weight Management: Striving for a healthy body weight helps the body respond better to its own insulin."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "2. Oral Medications"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Metformin: Universally used as the standard first-line oral medication to reduce glucose production in the liver and improve insulin sensitivity.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 Inhibitors (e.g., Empagliflozin, Canagliflozin): Help the kidneys remove excess glucose through urine while offering extra heart and kidney protection.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "DPP-4 Inhibitors (e.g., Sitagliptin): Oral pills that help increase insulin production and lower glucagon levels after meals."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Sulfonylureas (e.g., Gliclazide, Glimepiride): Stimulate the pancreas to release more insulin, though they carry a risk of low blood sugar (hypoglycemia) and weight gain."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Thiazolidinediones / Pioglitazone: Help muscle and fat cells respond better to insulin."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "3. Injectable Medications"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 Receptor Agonists (e.g., Dulaglutide, Liraglutide): Weekly or daily injections that mimic natural hormones to boost insulin release, slow digestion, reduce appetite, and support weight loss .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Insulin Therapy: Used when oral medications and non-insulin injectables are no longer enough to reach target blood glucose levels; delivered via pens, syringes, or continuous insulin pumps."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "4. Monitoring & Procedural Options"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood Sugar Monitoring: Using traditional glucose meters or wearable continuous glucose monitors (CGMs) to track daily trends."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metabolic Surgery: Weight loss surgery is sometimes considered for eligible individuals to achieve sustainable long-term glycemic control."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help tailor this information, are you asking for yourself or a loved one, and are you looking for details on a specific medication class or lifestyle guidance ?"
      }
    ],
